WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:24.000
Why do people spy on their own allies?

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.810
What motivates traitors?

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:31.029
US Intelligence officials have boiled down
the motives for treason to four possibilities

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:32.829
in the acronym ‘MICE’.

00:00:32.829 --> 00:00:34.760
M – Money.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:35.860
No explanation needed here.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.370
It’s a motive as old as humankind.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:40.370
I – Ideology.

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:42.940
This can be even more powerful than money.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:48.380
Sometimes in the name of misguided idealism,
secret agents can turn against their own countries,

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:52.110
as in the case of famous Soviet double agent
Kim Philby.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.690
C – Compromise.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:54.860
Blackmail or Coercion.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.570
An unwilling agent may be forced to hand over
secrets, lest compromising or embarrassing

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:00.860
details are divulged.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:01.860
E – Ego.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.000
The most dangerous and unpredictable reason.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.400
Playing two sides can provide excitement and
a feeling of power.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.780
Today’s protagonist, Robert Hanssen, was
an FBI agent who served his country for 20

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:14.780
years.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.350
At the end of the Cold War, Hanssen sold classified
secrets to the Soviet and Russian intelligence

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.350
services.

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:24.600
He compromised the security of his country
and put the lives of colleagues in danger.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.310
He realised his mistakes and stopped spying,
but eventually returned to his duplicitous

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.020
role with a vengeance.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.530
What were his motives?

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:37.520
Apparently, it was a combination of Money
and Ego, but there may have been a darker

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:43.820
demon lurking under the façade of Hanssen,
a devout Catholic and father of six.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:53.350
Let’s find out, in today’s Biographics
The Accountant

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:57.700
Robert Phillip Hanssen was born in Chicago,
Illinois, just weeks before D-Day, on April

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.280
18, 1944.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.520
On the day of his birth, his father was not
there, as he was serving in the US Navy during

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:04.520
World War II.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:08.610
In peacetime, Hanssen Sr was a Police Officer
in the Chicago Police Department.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:13.439
The old man was one who would not suffer fools,
neither in the streets, nor at home.

00:02:13.439 --> 00:02:17.599
As Robert grew up, his father was reported
to have frequently abused him, ranting that

00:02:17.599 --> 00:02:20.220
he was a failure and would never succeed in
life.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.940
After graduating from high school, Hanssen
attended Knox College in Illinois, where he

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.810
studied chemistry and Russian.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:29.499
After graduation, he considered becoming a
dentist, but eventually he set his eyes on

00:02:29.499 --> 00:02:30.970
completing an MBA.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:33.519
He began his career as an accountant.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:38.190
In 1968 Robert married his fiancée, Bernadette
Wauck, known as ‘Bonnie’.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.969
Bonnie, a Catholic, was born in a family that
was much wealthier than Robert’s.

00:02:41.969 --> 00:02:47.419
Influenced by her devotion, he converted to
the same faith and joined Opus Dei, an exclusive,

00:02:47.419 --> 00:02:49.060
conservative order within the Church.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.799
The new couple had six children and committed
to send each of them to exclusive private

00:02:53.799 --> 00:02:54.799
schools.

00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:58.299
In accordance with Opus Dei tradition, they
would also commit to donate a portion of their

00:02:58.299 --> 00:03:03.590
income to the charitable works of their parish.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:09.749
The Agent
This tithing lifestyle surely took its toll

00:03:09.749 --> 00:03:11.400
on Robert’s paycheck.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.780
Strangely, after a few years working as an
accountant, he decided to enter law enforcement,

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.879
which generally tends to be a less lucrative
career.

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:22.829
Regardless, Hanssen joined the forced and
worked as a policeman in Chicago for four

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:23.829
years.

00:03:23.829 --> 00:03:27.209
Thanks to his background, Robert was placed
on an elite unit investigating corruption

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:28.779
and white-collar crimes.

00:03:28.779 --> 00:03:33.889
After this stint with the boys in blue, Robert
decided he would join those in grey: the FBI.

00:03:33.889 --> 00:03:37.069
At this stage he had the right CV to join
the agency.

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:41.939
His credentials as an accountant with an MBA,
plus four years in the Chicago anti-corruption

00:03:41.939 --> 00:03:45.489
squad made him a good fit for the agency’s
Financial Crimes Division.

00:03:45.489 --> 00:03:50.639
He was assigned to the Division’s headquarters
in Gary, Indiana, right out of the FBI Academy.

00:03:50.639 --> 00:03:54.969
But even as Hanssen investigated white-collar
criminals in the Midwest, he was dreaming

00:03:54.969 --> 00:03:56.480
of something different.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:03:58.959
Hanssen had grown up with a fondness for espionage.

00:03:58.959 --> 00:04:02.730
He was a huge James Bond fan, devouring Ian
Fleming’s books as well as the movies.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:03.730
He had even bought a Walther PPK pistol – a
Bond favourite -- plus a Leica miniature camera,

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:04.730
and a shortwave radio.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:05.730
For some reason, he had even opened a Swiss
bank account.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:11.059
Unfortunately for Hannssen, 007 remained nothing
more than a number on one of his spreadsheets.

00:04:11.059 --> 00:04:14.489
Hanssen, though, had reasons to hope for a
more exciting assignment.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:19.669
In 1978, he filed a transfer request to the
FBI office in New York City, one of the great

00:04:19.669 --> 00:04:21.419
spy capitals of the world.

00:04:21.419 --> 00:04:25.360
He was assigned to the Soviet Counterintelligence
Analytical Squad.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.210
It seemed like adventure might finally be
calling.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:29.210
But it wasn’t so.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:33.180
You see, Hannssen’s supervisors described
him as smart, technically proficient, and

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.180
analytical.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:37.490
They marvelled at his skills with early computer
systems.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:43.090
But they also considered him lacking in field
operations ability and assigned him to administrative

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:44.090
work.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:47.880
This crushed his fantasies of going undercover,
chasing Soviet spies in the underbelly of

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:48.880
New York.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.910
Hanssen’s job was to manage the New York
Field Office’s counterintelligence database.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:57.750
He had joined the FBI to hunt spies, but the
agency had made him a glorified librarian.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:01.280
In all honesty, the job description sounds
quite cool if you ask me.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.250
Hanssen’s job was to assemble a database
of foreign officials posted in New York who,

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:10.060
while posing as diplomats, were actually intelligence
officers spying on the United States.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:14.870
Many of them were agents of the main Soviet
intelligence agency, the KGB, or its military

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:18.940
counterpart: a larger but less famous agency,
the GRU.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:25.980
This just wasn’t enough for Robert Hanssen.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:30.220
The Double Agent
At this stage, Special Agent Hanssen felt

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:31.650
wronged by the FBI.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:35.020
He also felt the increasing weight of the
bills piling up.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:38.710
His growing family needed more space, and
Hanssen’s traditional stance required him

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:42.210
to be the main bread-winner, requiring Bonnie
to stay at home.

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:47.090
These may have been the reasons that pushed
him to the edge of treason for the first time.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:51.830
In November 1979, Hanssen used his growing
knowledge of Soviet operations to contact

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:54.330
the GRU and volunteer his services.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:59.440
Clever and stealthy from the start, Hanssen
concealed his identity from his Soviet contacts.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:05.650
From 1979 until 1981, he left information
stolen from his department, the Soviet Counterintelligence

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:10.830
Unit in dead drops in exchange for a sum of
money that sources say fell somewhere between

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:14.240
$21,000 to $30,000.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:16.180
That’s about $60 to 90,000 in today’s
money.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.680
Dead drops are a common method used by spies
to exchange information.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.460
Spy No. 1 drops his or her messages in an
agreed, hidden place -- behind a loose brick

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:26.140
on a certain wall, for example.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:31.340
The spy then leaves a commonly understood
sign, such as a simple chalk mark, on another

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:32.340
place.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:35.920
The handlers then know that they have mail
waiting and can pick up the bundle.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.120
The intel that Hanssen provided to the Soviets
was highly valuable.

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.490
Hanssen even revealed the name of a Russian
general, Dimitri Polyakov, who had been providing

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:45.110
information to the Americans.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:49.250
Polyakov was placed under surveillance by
the Russians; he was eventually arrested as

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.330
a spy and executed in 1988.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:07:01.200
The Remorseful Spy
The GRU experience was a positive test for

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.500
Hanssen’s resolve and ability.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:06.770
He might have continued his work for them,
but in the spring of 1980, Hanssen was caught.

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:12.930
One can hide secrets from the FBI, the GRU,
the KGB even – but not from a spouse.

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:16.240
Bonnie found a strange letter from the GRU
to Hanssen in the basement of their house

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:18.270
in Scarsdale, New York.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.430
Hanssen downplayed it.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:23.520
He told Bonnie that he had been giving the
Soviets junk information, to trick them into

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:24.520
giving him money.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:28.340
In a later testimony, Bonnie said that she
demanded that her husband go with her to see

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:30.840
their Roman Catholic priest to confess.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:35.810
They then drove to New Rochelle to meet with
Rev. Robert Bucciarelli, a priest also associated

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:37.340
with Opus Dei.

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:41.120
Father Bucciarelli came up with a plan: if
Robert gave the money from the Soviets to

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.260
charity, and promised not to spy again, as
a good boy, the priest committed not to report

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:46.260
the matter to the FBI.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:50.960
Which in theory he shouldn’t have done anyways,
as confessions are protected by an oath of

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.340
secrecy, but … whatever.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:54.340
Hanssen agreed.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:57.900
Bonnie, who at the time was expecting their
fourth child, was greatly relieved, but she

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.640
was also determined to hold her husband to
the deal.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:04.700
Robert admitted that he had already spent
most of the GRU money, but Bonnie insisted

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:07.690
that he repay to charity the full original
amount.

00:08:07.690 --> 00:08:11.680
He began to make small payments over several
years to a charity affiliated with Mother

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:15.880
Teresa's Catholic organization, which moved
the family close to bankruptcy.

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:19.880
Over the following years, Bonnie kept close
tabs on Robert, checking if he was making

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:23.440
the payments, and even questioning him on
whether he was still working with the Russians.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:27.210
It seems to me like the FBI would have been
wise to hire Bonnie Hannssen.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:32.050
In the early 1980s, Hanssen was transferred
to FBI headquarters in Washington, D.C.

00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:35.779
To his colleagues in the bureau, who were
still oblivious to his actual conduct, he

00:08:35.779 --> 00:08:37.110
seemed to be a model agent.

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:41.510
In office chit-chat, Hanssen often spoke of
his engagement with Opus Dei and appeared

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:44.339
to be a devoted Catholic and anti-communist.

00:08:44.339 --> 00:08:49.700
From 1981 until 1985, he served in the Budget
and Soviet Analytical Units at FBI headquarters.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:54.740
He may still have been a desk jockey, but
one nested at the centre of a web of information

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:57.699
flowing to and from the intelligence division.

00:08:57.699 --> 00:09:02.020
As one of the few skilled computer operators,
Hannssen’s cyber tendrils took in information

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:07.309
from the NSA and the CIA, reviewing info on
the FBI’s most sensitive human assets and

00:09:07.309 --> 00:09:09.610
technical operations against the USSR.

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:14.920
Finally, Hanssen realized that the best spies
were not flashy James Bonds, but rather, the

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:21.629
smart men and women who lurked in the shadows,
manipulating information, unnoticed and unknown.

00:09:21.629 --> 00:09:25.120
In September 1985, Hanssen returned to the
New York Field Office.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:30.070
This time he supervised a foreign counterintelligence
squad, which gave him access to a wealth of

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:31.569
Soviet spy information.

00:09:31.569 --> 00:09:36.180
This included the identity of the FBI’s
Soviet sources and informants, as well as

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.779
the locations of KGB defectors in the US.

00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:44.230
Emboldened by his former success with the
GRU, Hanssen graduated to the big league.

00:09:44.230 --> 00:10:05.829
He was going to spy

00:10:05.829 --> 00:10:32.720
for

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.899
the KGB.

00:10:35.899 --> 00:11:41.459
The Unrepentant Spy

00:11:41.459 --> 00:11:45.519
In October 4, 1985, Soviet diplomat Viktor
Degtyar received a strange looking envelope

00:11:45.519 --> 00:11:48.209
in his house in Alexandria, Virginia.

00:11:48.209 --> 00:11:51.899
Degtyar opened the envelope and found a second
sealed envelope inside.

00:11:51.899 --> 00:11:56.880
A note with instructions forbade Degtyar from
opening the second envelope and demanded he

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:58.600
take it to his colleague Victor Cherkashin.

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:03.490
It was clear to Degtyar that whoever had sent
the envelope was aware that he was, in fact,

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:05.370
a KGB officer of the PR Line.

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:10.079
The KGB organized its espionage activities
into distinct lines, or sections, with different

00:12:10.079 --> 00:12:13.019
intelligence and counterintelligence responsibilities.

00:12:13.019 --> 00:12:18.540
The ‘PR’ collected information about political,
economic, and military strategic intelligence.

00:12:18.540 --> 00:12:22.860
Each line of activity came under the direction
of the KGB rezident, an official member of

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.490
the Soviet Embassy who was in fact a covert
spymaster.

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:28.759
Cherkashin was Viktor’s rezident.

00:12:28.759 --> 00:12:33.699
The next day Degtyar handed over the package
to Cherkashin, a veteran spy who had also

00:12:33.699 --> 00:12:37.949
been the handler of Aldrich Ames, the CIA’s
most dangerous mole.

00:12:37.949 --> 00:12:39.999
The letter read
“Dear Mr. Cherkashin:

00:12:39.999 --> 00:12:42.920
Soon, I will send a box of documents to Mr.
Degtyar.

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:47.809
They are from certain of the most sensitive
and highly compartmented projects of the U.S.

00:12:47.809 --> 00:12:48.809
Intelligence community.

00:12:48.809 --> 00:12:51.999
All are originals to aid in Verifying their
authenticity.

00:12:51.999 --> 00:12:53.970
…
I trust that an officer of your experience

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:55.329
will handle them appropriately.

00:12:55.329 --> 00:12:59.230
I believe they are sufficient to justify a
$100,000 payment to me.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:03.940
I must warn of certain risks to your security
of which you may not be aware.

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:05.899
Your service has recently suffered some setbacks.

00:13:05.899 --> 00:13:06.899
I warn that

00:13:06.899 --> 00:13:10.209
Mr. Boris Yuzhin
Mr. Sergey Motorin and

00:13:10.209 --> 00:13:14.569
Mr. Valeriy Martynov
have been recruited by our special services.

00:13:14.569 --> 00:13:19.370
My identity and actual position in the community
must be left unstated to ensure my security.

00:13:19.370 --> 00:13:24.959
I will add 6, (you Subtract 6) from stated
months, days and times in both directions

00:13:24.959 --> 00:13:26.740
of our future communications.”

00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:30.059
And then, as a signature, a simple
“B”

00:13:30.059 --> 00:13:31.990
Cherkashin had struck gold.

00:13:31.990 --> 00:13:36.029
Three double agents exposed in one stroke,
simply by opening an envelope.

00:13:36.029 --> 00:13:39.110
And Hanssen had scored a 100 grand.

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:42.389
His first letter to Cherkashin had also caused
enormous damage.

00:13:42.389 --> 00:13:47.920
The FBI and CIA relied on Yuzhin, Motorin,
and Martynov to spy for the US and provide

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:50.999
critical inside information about Soviet espionage
operations.

00:13:50.999 --> 00:13:54.999
After Hanssen’s letter, the KGB flew each
of the spies back to Moscow and tried them

00:13:54.999 --> 00:13:56.230
for espionage.

00:13:56.230 --> 00:13:59.800
After sham trials, the Soviets executed Motorin
and Martynov.

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.290
Yuzhin received a fifteen-year sentence.

00:14:02.290 --> 00:14:07.550
From 1985 to 1991, Hanssen handed to the KGB
some of the most important counterintelligence

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:11.259
and military secrets in the hands of US security
services.

00:14:11.259 --> 00:14:16.189
By 1991, Hanssen had completely compromised
the FBI’s counter-intelligence database.

00:14:16.189 --> 00:14:20.499
Meanwhile, Hanssen was counting his money
at his FBI desk, laughing while the agency

00:14:20.499 --> 00:14:22.980
looked for the mole in all the wrong places.

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:25.290
He could have easily been caught at this time.

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:29.709
During a visit from her brother, Mark Wauck,
Bonnie had confided to him that she was alarmed.

00:14:29.709 --> 00:14:33.920
She had found $5,000 in cash at home, and
feared that Robert may have started spying

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:35.309
for the Russians again.

00:14:35.309 --> 00:14:38.019
Mark was an FBI agent himself, although based
in Chicago.

00:14:38.019 --> 00:14:43.309
He reported this to his supervisors in 1990,
but they didn’t take any meaningful action!

00:14:43.309 --> 00:14:48.600
In the following years Robert managed to avoid
detection, as he skilfully worked with the

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:50.060
KGB handlers without ever meeting them.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:53.779
In one occasion, he slipped up, speaking to
a Russian agent over a pay phone – this

00:14:53.779 --> 00:14:57.989
could have easily given him away, as presumably
all Soviet Embassy lines were tapped.

00:14:57.989 --> 00:15:00.050
But again, nothing happened.

00:15:00.050 --> 00:15:04.181
In general, Hanssen and Degtyar relied on
a system of dead drops and signals placed

00:15:04.181 --> 00:15:05.209
in public places.

00:15:05.209 --> 00:15:09.929
For instance, a piece of adhesive tape placed
on a sign in a park in Virginia would indicate

00:15:09.929 --> 00:15:13.220
that a package had been placed under a small
footbridge in the park.

00:15:13.220 --> 00:15:15.619
In 1991, the Soviet Union collapsed.

00:15:15.619 --> 00:15:17.220
This was bad news for Hanssen.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:21.649
During the early 1990s, KGB veterans began
to approach western intelligence agencies

00:15:21.649 --> 00:15:24.559
and spill the beans on their recent operations.

00:15:24.559 --> 00:15:28.019
Hanssen became alarmed: what if a Russian
with knowledge of his activities signaled

00:15:28.019 --> 00:15:30.040
the Americans to his true identity?

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:33.540
For eight years, Hanssen behaved liked a boy
scout – at least spy-wise.

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:37.519
He stopped contacting the Russians, while
waiting for the inevitable arrest.

00:15:37.519 --> 00:15:39.509
Again, nothing happened.

00:15:39.509 --> 00:15:44.019
So, in 1999, while working as an FBI liaison
with the State Department, he fell back into

00:15:44.019 --> 00:15:46.949
bad habits and started selling American secrets
again.

00:15:46.949 --> 00:15:51.070
Little did he know that a former colleague
on the other side of the former Iron Curtain

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:53.420
had begun doing exactly the same thing.

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:58.569
A former KGB agent had come across Hanssen's
KGB file and contacted US intelligence with

00:15:58.569 --> 00:16:00.339
the intention of selling it.

00:16:00.339 --> 00:16:05.019
Realizing the importance of the material,
the US paid $7 million for it.

00:16:05.019 --> 00:16:11.149
In 20 years of selling classified intel, Hanssen
had racked up approximately $1.4 Million dollars.

00:16:11.149 --> 00:16:15.179
This Russian guy, in perhaps two months of
work, had made $7 Million!

00:16:15.179 --> 00:16:20.429
Either the Americans had deeper pockets, or
Hanssen was a terrible negotiator.

00:16:20.429 --> 00:16:23.949
The dossier sold to the Americans did not
specify the mole’s name.

00:16:23.949 --> 00:16:27.799
Evidence in the file, though, pointed to Hanssen,
and he was put under close surveillance from

00:16:27.799 --> 00:16:31.750
Special Agent O’Neill, a younger colleague
who posed as his assistant.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:37.600
The net was closing in.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:43.759
Hanssen’s Demons
On February 18, 2001, Hanssen was arrested

00:16:43.759 --> 00:16:48.639
at a park in northern Virginia after he had
placed a package at his usual dead drop location.

00:16:48.639 --> 00:16:53.309
The evidence against him was overwhelming,
and to avoid the death penalty, Hanssen confessed

00:16:53.309 --> 00:16:57.199
and agreed to be debriefed by American intelligence
officials.

00:16:57.199 --> 00:17:00.009
In May 2002, Hanssen was sentenced to life
in prison.

00:17:00.009 --> 00:17:04.230
News reports claimed that intelligence agencies
were not entirely satisfied with the extent

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:07.610
of his cooperation -- they believed he was
holding back information.

00:17:07.610 --> 00:17:11.589
But the Government could not prove he had
lied, and wishing to avoid a public trial,

00:17:11.589 --> 00:17:13.570
they did not void his plea agreement.

00:17:13.570 --> 00:17:17.490
The Hanssen case was regarded as a low point
for the FBI.

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:20.690
Hanssen had been trusted by his superiors,
he could have been stopped in more than one

00:17:20.690 --> 00:17:24.570
occasion and his betrayal had gone undetected
for years.

00:17:24.570 --> 00:17:28.930
The Government stated that Hanssen had been
paid approximately $1.4 million during his

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:32.610
spying career, although most of it was held
in a Russian bank account.

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:35.230
The damage Hanssen did was catastrophic.

00:17:35.230 --> 00:17:38.980
At least three Russian double agents had been
executed or imprisoned because of him, and

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:42.160
it was suspected that he compromised dozens
of intelligence operations.

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:46.560
For example, he had sold the information that
the Americans had dug a tunnel under the Russian

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:50.010
embassy in Washington, to install listening
devices.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:54.000
Hanssen was incarcerated in the "supermax"
federal prison in Colorado.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:57.780
Fellow convicts include the Unabomber, one
of the Boston Marathon bombers, and a number

00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:59.690
of organized crime figures.

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:03.980
But why did Hanssen betray his country?

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:08.330
During his sessions with investigators, Hanssen
claimed his motivation had always been financial.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:13.010
Yet some investigators believed anger about
how his father treated him as a child triggered

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.890
a need to rebel against authority.

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:19.040
Author Eric O’Neill, on the other hand,
speculated that his motives lay in his egomania

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:20.570
and thirst for adventure.

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:23.880
The man himself told his investigators what
had sparked his spying:

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.100
“Fear and rage.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:30.360
Fear of being a failure and fear of not being
able to provide for my family.”

00:18:30.360 --> 00:18:34.810
Hanssen said he felt “rage” towards the
FBI each time he was denied a promotion.

00:18:34.810 --> 00:18:38.380
He was determined to prove they had made a
mistake in not promoting him by pulling off

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:41.140
increasingly large-scale feats of espionage.

00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:44.770
Hanssen was convinced that other FBI agents
lacked his commitment to the bureau.

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:48.580
And the bureau, in his view, was not fighting
the Russians in the right way.

00:18:48.580 --> 00:18:52.810
As reporters dug deeper into the case, more
disturbing psychological demons started to

00:18:52.810 --> 00:18:53.810
emerge.

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:57.870
Friends of Hanssen told journalists that he
had exhibited eccentric behaviour, which included

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:01.310
an obsession with pornography and sex in general.

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:05.400
Lesley Stahl from CBS reported that Hanssen
had taken the habit of sending nude pictures

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:06.900
of his wife to a friend of his.

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:11.190
He even posted sexually explicit online stories
about Bonnie on the Internet.

00:19:11.190 --> 00:19:14.950
Hanssen got to the point of inviting his friend
to watch a live stream of him having sex with

00:19:14.950 --> 00:19:17.590
Bonnie on a secretly installed CCTV system.

00:19:17.590 --> 00:19:20.030
Bonnie knew nothing about any of this.

00:19:20.030 --> 00:19:24.600
Hanssen's erratic behaviour escalated, including
numerous visits to strip clubs, where he tried

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.670
to convert strippers to Catholicism.

00:19:26.670 --> 00:19:30.980
Psychiatrist Alan Salerian was hired – and
later fired - by Hanssen's defense team to

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:32.040
examine him.

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:36.240
According to Dr Salerian, spying for the Soviets,
for the devoutly religious Hanssen, was a

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:39.440
way to escape those sexual problems.

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:42.570
"[Hanssen's] espionage was an escape from
his sexual demons.

00:19:42.570 --> 00:19:47.060
When he found himself in exciting, dangerous
positions, such as espionage and spying, he

00:19:47.060 --> 00:19:48.630
found that his demons slowed down.

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:52.360
He is driven by demons…by thoughts, unwanted
thoughts."

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:56.860
Dr. Salerian, who spent 30 hours interviewing
Hanssen, concluded that the double agent was

00:19:56.860 --> 00:19:57.860
no amoral.

00:19:57.860 --> 00:20:02.550
He was simply an ill man, suffering from a
"severe psychological disorder" of sexual

00:20:02.550 --> 00:20:09.650
nature, who could find respite from his torments
only by putting himself in risky situations.

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:17.870
MICE … ?
In 2007 Special Agent O’Neill, instrumental

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:22.730
in Hanssen’s capture, worked with director
and screen writer Billy Ray on the film “Breach”,

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:25.930
a realistic portrayal of the last year of
Hanssen’s career.

00:20:25.930 --> 00:20:31.110
Author Eric O’Neill published ‘Gray Day’
in 2013, also about Hanssen’s story, in

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:34.340
which he is depicted as America’s first
Cyber Spy.

00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:38.410
Spies, secret agents, and the intelligence
community will always capture our imagination,

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:43.170
especially when their activities appear to
be outside of the boundaries of our accepted

00:20:43.170 --> 00:20:44.170
morality.

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:48.860
What can lead a man to sell and betray his
colleagues and the security of his country?

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:52.831
If you look at the MICE set of motives, most
of the time it can be because of money; or

00:20:52.831 --> 00:20:58.080
a true belief in lofty ideal; or maybe he
has been blackmailed and strong-armed; or

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:01.490
maybe it is just down to one’s ego and the
need to feed it.

00:21:01.490 --> 00:21:06.030
Hanssen’s betrayal ultimately may have been
down to his ego, but not as a way to satisfy

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:10.420
it, rather to placate those obsessions he
could not control.

00:21:10.420 --> 00:21:13.810
We hinted at another ‘mole’ or traitor
in our video today, but please let us know

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:18.390
if you would like to hear the story of another
double agent we may have not heard of.

00:21:18.390 --> 00:21:31.660
Comment till your fingers drop, share, like,
subscribe and as usual … thank you for watching!

